from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('1.1')
t.claim('01','Resources are limited relative to the wants that could be satisfied with them.','definition')
t.claim('01','Scarcity concerns competing uses of productive resources; an unsold finished good does not establish that those resources are unlimited.')
t.claim('01','An increase in available resources can widen feasible choices without eliminating all competing wants.')
t.claim('02','Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone when a choice is made.','definition')
t.claim('02','A next-best alternative must be feasible, and its forgone value need not be a money payment.')
t.claim('02','Moving along a production possibility frontier with given resources and technology illustrates output sacrificed to obtain more of another output.')
t.claim('03','A reasoned choice compares benefits with the next-best feasible sacrifice in relation to a stated objective.','evaluative')
t.claim('03','Changes in constraints or in the value of alternatives can alter a justified decision.','evaluative')
t.claim('03','Removing one specific resource conflict does not prove that every wider opportunity cost has disappeared.','evaluative')
t.block('01','definition','Scarcity creates choices',"""
Resources are limited relative to the wants that could be satisfied with them. This is scarcity, the basic economic problem. A household may want safer transport, improved meals and money for future study. Available income limits the purchases it can make now. At the level of production, land, people’s working time, equipment and materials restrict the goods and services that can be supplied. Resources used for one purpose cannot simultaneously perform every competing use. Choices therefore arise even when people have some income and producers have useful equipment.

Scarcity is a relationship between resources and competing wants, rather than a synonym for having no possessions. More resources can make additional choices possible, but new or previously unmet wants can remain. An invented repair workshop in Tsumeb might buy another workbench and still have more repair requests than its mechanics can complete. The improvement matters; it simply does not demonstrate that all resource constraints have disappeared.
""")
t.block('01','misconception','Scarcity and unsold products',"""
Suppose that workshop has unused bicycle tyres in stock. It would be wrong to infer that tyres, mechanics’ time and storage space are unlimited. The stock might remain because customers want another tyre size, cannot afford the price, or do not need repairs this week. Unsold goods concern what happened in a particular market. Resource scarcity concerns whether competing uses exceed what resources can achieve. A mechanic could repair a bicycle, service a handcart or train an apprentice during the same hour. The unused stock does not remove that choice. Ask which resources still have alternative uses before deciding whether scarcity remains.
""")
t.block('02','plain_explanation','Identify the next-best feasible alternative',"""
Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone when a choice is made. First identify whose decision is being considered. Then identify the chosen use and the best feasible alternative that it rules out. Do not add the benefits of every rejected option. Those alternatives may exclude one another too. A person with one free evening cannot give up three separate full-evening activities that could never have been undertaken together.

The sacrifice is a benefit forgone, which need not equal a cash payment. If a learner chooses revision instead of a walk with friends, and that walk is the next-best feasible activity, the opportunity cost is the enjoyment or companionship forgone. The revision may cost no money. Feasibility also matters: an unaffordable holiday cannot be the relevant alternative to a small purchase when the holiday lay outside the available budget.
""")
t.block('02','worked_application','An original production illustration',"""
Use these invented weekly possibilities for a small workshop with fixed working time and equipment. It can complete combinations of bicycle services and handcart services: (0,8), (2,6), (4,4), (6,2) and (8,0). Put bicycle services on the horizontal axis and handcart services on the vertical axis, with both measured per week. Plot the five efficient combinations and join them to obtain a straight production possibility frontier.

Moving from four bicycle services and four handcart services to six bicycle services and two handcart services gains two bicycle services but sacrifices two handcart services. The opportunity cost is one handcart service for each extra bicycle service over this interval. Resources and technology have not increased: their use has changed. The straight line represents constant opportunity cost in this deliberately simple example. A point inside the frontier indicates that these stated possibilities are not fully used; a point beyond it cannot be achieved with the given resources and technology. The illustration describes this workshop’s choices, not a measured national frontier.
""")
t.block('03','analysis_chain','Compare benefits and sacrifices',"""
Opportunity cost makes the sacrifice behind a decision explicit, but does not rank everyone’s priorities for them. Imagine the fictional Sand River Youth Hub in Rundu. It can staff one afternoon activity: mathematics tutoring or bicycle-maintenance training. Its stated priority is helping learners with an urgent mathematics assessment, so it ranks tutoring first. Bicycle training is feasible and ranks second. Choosing tutoring supports the priority but gives up the benefit of the training session.

A developed explanation connects the constraint to the sacrifice. One staffed session cannot accommodate both activities. Selecting tutoring uses that session, making training unavailable then. Because training is the best feasible rejected activity, its benefit supplies the opportunity cost. Calling the hub’s N$800 materials budget the opportunity cost would skip this comparison: the cash budget and the forgone activity are different pieces of information.
""")
t.block('03','evaluation','A conditional judgement',"""
There is a case for tutoring because it directly addresses the hub’s urgent priority. There is a case against the choice because practical bicycle skills also have value for the applicants. Tutoring is justified if the urgency of mathematics support outweighs the training benefits under the stated objective. That conclusion identifies whose priority determines the decision, rather than claiming one activity is better for everyone.

Now suppose an additional volunteer and another afternoon become available for bicycle training without reducing tutoring. The original conflict can change: the hub may be able to provide both activities. Before concluding that the revised arrangement is preferable, check whether volunteer time, rooms or materials would displace another valued use. A changed constraint can alter the choice, but it does not prove that all opportunity costs everywhere have vanished. Likewise, new information about applicants’ needs could alter the ranking even if resources stayed unchanged.
""")
t.block('01 02 03','cross_link','From individual choices to wider allocation',"""
Topic 1.2 explains the factors of production being allocated and the economic systems coordinating their use. The same opportunity-cost reasoning later helps evaluate government policy in topic 6.2 and conflicts of interest in topic 9.1. Keep the decision-maker, period and constraint consistent. A household’s sacrifice may differ from a producer’s sacrifice, while a policy can affect several groups. In each case begin with the feasible alternative actually forgone, then explain the benefits and conditions supporting the judgement.
""")
t.card('DEF','01','Define scarcity.','Resources are limited relative to the wants that could be satisfied with them.', ['Meaning/definition and essential element (1): the relationship between limited resources and competing wants; credit the complete meaning.'])
t.card('FEATURE','01','Identify two features that together create the basic economic problem.','Available resources are limited; wants competing for their use exceed what they can satisfy.', ['Characteristic (1): limited resources. Distinction/example of the second feature (1): competing wants exceed what those resources can satisfy.'])
t.card('MISCON','01','Explain why unsold bicycle tyres at an invented Tsumeb repair workshop do not establish that the economy has no scarcity.','Unsold tyres show that those particular products have not all been bought. They do not show unlimited resources: mechanics’ time, materials and space still have competing uses.', ['Confusion/boundary (1): unsold finished products do not imply unlimited productive resources. Test to distinguish (1): check whether resources still have competing feasible uses.'])
t.card('DIST','02','Explain the difference between opportunity cost and the combined value of every rejected option.','Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best feasible alternative forgone. Combining all rejected options counts alternatives that may not have been available together.', ['Difference (1): next-best feasible alternative rather than all alternatives. Contrast/test (1): mutually exclusive alternatives cannot all have been chosen instead.'])
t.card('APP','02','Explain the opportunity cost when a learner chooses an hour of revision instead of a walk with friends. The walk is the next-best feasible use of that hour.','The opportunity cost is the enjoyment or companionship forgone from the walk, because it is the next-best feasible alternative ruled out by revision.', ['Context (1): benefit of the specified walk forgone. Reason/conclusion (1): it is the next-best feasible alternative excluded by the revision choice.'])
t.card('DIAGRAM','02','Explain opportunity cost using a production possibility frontier for an invented Namibian workshop. Weekly feasible combinations (bicycle services, handcart services) are (0,8), (2,6), (4,4), (6,2), (8,0). Resources and technology are fixed and each combination uses them efficiently. Plot bicycles horizontally and handcarts vertically. Explain moving from (4,4) to (6,2).','Label both axes in services per week and join the five combinations with a straight frontier. Moving from (4,4) to (6,2) gains two bicycle services and sacrifices two handcart services. The opportunity cost is one handcart service per extra bicycle service. This is movement along the existing frontier, not increased productive capacity.', ['Axes/labels (1): bicycle and handcart services per week. Initial position/original curve (2): correct five combinations (1), straight frontier through them (1). Change/control (1): (4,4) to (6,2) with unchanged resources. Explanation (2): two handcart services sacrificed (1) to gain two bicycle services (1). Interpretation (1): one handcart service per extra bicycle service. Total 7.'])
base='Invented teaching situation: Sand River Youth Hub in Rundu can staff one afternoon activity, mathematics tutoring or bicycle-maintenance training. Both have applicants. Its N$800 materials budget is sufficient for either activity, but staffing limits it to one. It prioritises urgent mathematics support and ranks tutoring first, bicycle training second.'
t.card('EVAL','03','Evaluate the hub’s choice of tutoring. '+base,'Tutoring addresses the stated urgent priority and therefore puts the scarce staffed session to its preferred use. However, applicants lose the valuable bicycle-training opportunity. Tutoring is justified if the urgency of mathematics support outweighs that sacrifice under the hub’s objective. A new volunteer and time slot could change feasibility, so the judgement should be revisited if the training could then run without displacing another valued use.', ['Argument for (2): meets mathematics priority (1), using the scarce staffed session to address urgent need (1). Argument against (2): training benefit forgone (1), reducing opportunities for its applicants (1). Condition/trade-off (1): relative urgency under the stated objective. Judgement (2): choose tutoring on that priority (1), reconsider if the constraint changes and both become feasible (1). Total 7.'],[base])
p1=t.wo['performance_tasks'][0]
t.card(p1,'01 02','Define scarcity (2). Identify one scarce resource in a repair workshop and one feasible alternative use (2). Explain opportunity cost (2). Identify the non-monetary benefit forgone when a learner chooses revision over a walk, with the walk stated to be the next-best feasible activity (2).','Scarcity means limited resources relative to competing wants. Mechanics’ time can service bicycles or handcarts. Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best feasible alternative forgone. The learner sacrifices the enjoyment or companionship of the walk, which the stated ranking makes the relevant alternative.', ['Meaning (2): limited resources (1), relative to competing wants (1). Identification, separate points (2): mechanics’ time (1), handcart servicing as an alternative to bicycle servicing (1). Explanation (2): next-best feasible alternative (1), its value forgone (1). Final identification (2): enjoyment/companionship of the walk (1), tied to the supplied next-best activity (1). Marks allocation totals 8.'])
p2=t.wo['performance_tasks'][1]
t.card(p2,'01 02 03',base+' Describe the resource constraint, choice and opportunity cost (6). Explain why the next-best feasible activity, rather than every possible activity added together, supplies the opportunity cost (7). Discuss the decision if an additional volunteer and a second afternoon become available for training without reducing tutoring, while other demands on these resources must still be checked (7).','The staffed afternoon is limited, both activities are wanted, and the hub selects tutoring. Bicycle training is feasible and ranks next, so its benefit is forgone. The same staffed session cannot support both activities. Selecting tutoring excludes training then; the stated ranking establishes training as the best alternative. Adding other possible activities would aggregate mutually exclusive uses rather than identify what could replace tutoring. Tutoring remains valuable under the urgent-support priority. Training also benefits applicants, so the extra volunteer and afternoon could make both activities preferable to choosing only one. That judgement depends on confirming that the additional time, room and materials do not displace another more valued use.', ['Context/data description (6): scarce staffed afternoon (1), two wanted activities (1), tutoring chosen (1), training feasible (1), training next-ranked (1), training benefit forgone (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): shared staff/time cannot support both (1); selection excludes training then (1); training could replace tutoring (1); ranking makes it best alternative (1); opportunity cost values its benefit (1); other activities may be mutually exclusive (1); aggregating them misstates the feasible sacrifice (1). Both sides and judgement (7): tutoring serves priority (1), urgent need supports it (1); training has benefits (1), applicants gain if it runs (1); new resources change feasibility (1); prefer both if other uses are not displaced (1); check that condition before concluding (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
p3=t.wo['performance_tasks'][2]
t.card(p3,'03','Evaluate two decisions in separate response plans, seven marks each. '+base+' Case 1 uses the original one-session constraint. Case 2 adds a volunteer and a second afternoon for training without reducing tutoring, but the hub must check other demands on these resources. Plan an argument for, an argument against, a decisive condition and a judgement for each case.','Case 1: tutoring serves urgent mathematics needs; training benefits are sacrificed. Relative urgency under the stated objective decides whether tutoring is justified. Choose tutoring if that priority outweighs the training sacrifice. Case 2: providing both activities can retain both benefits with the additional volunteer and afternoon. Other demands on the new resources remain a possible sacrifice. Support the revised combination only if those uses do not displace something more valuable under the hub’s objective.', ['Case 1 marks (7): argument for meets priority (1) and urgent need (1); argument against loses training (1) and applicants’ skill benefits (1); condition is relative urgency (1); judgement chooses tutoring (1) because that condition matches the objective (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for retains tutoring (1) and adds training (1); argument against uses volunteer time (1) and another room/time opportunity (1); condition is no displacement of a more valued use (1); judgement favours both (1) only after checking that condition (1). Total 14; each plan totals 7.'],[base])
t.finish([{'from':'Performance scenarios specify reading support versus computer skills, as used in the structural exemplar.','to':'Original mathematics tutoring versus bicycle-maintenance training at an invented Rundu hub, with the same scarcity/ranking/changed-constraint demands.','why':'Avoid reusing the exemplar scenario while preserving all objective mappings, commands, tariffs, AO allocations and economic demands.'}])
